    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d901">

                <pb facs="0810" n="798" xml:id="pg798"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.248/590: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-06-22T19:00:00+03:00">June 22, 1942—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:22 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>655. Following telegram has been sent to Cairo.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>Your June 14, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="96" xml:id="d901-f1"/> received today.</p>
                    <p>Please thank Major General Maxwell, Colonels Halverson and Craw for me and
                        inform them I do not know of any assistance they could render at this time
                        in connection with negotiations arising out of landing of United States Army
                        planes and crews in Turkey. I am of opinion Colonel Craw’s arrival in Ankara
                        at this time might be harmful rather than helpful as it would tend to
                        magnify the incident which Turks have thus far joined us in minimizing.</p>
                    <p>In connection with any future flights that may be contemplated please invite
                        Craw’s attention to the fact that “release” of belligerent planes and crews
                        that land in neutral Turkey is not a matter of right but is governed by
                        clearly defined rules of international law on the subject.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
